    {
      "id": "956190377",
      "uuid": "10daeb1d-26c1-42b9-baec-211582be4297",
      "file_key": "MXbHIPIMANW2iIMvHeCSCR",
      "parent_id": "",
      "user": {
        "handle": "Nicole Weber",
        "img_url": "https://s3-alpha.figma.com/profile/659fa807-45cc-4f21-ba58-6677e8454ddf",
        "id": "862656244535756550"
      },
      "created_at": "2024-09-19T19:17:42.052Z",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "message": "@Rob Wu from your Slack message: What I'd also like to see for comparison is a new document with a flow where the user has to manually interact with about:addons to toggle a checkbox (e.g. \"Allow extensions to manage user scripts\"). This flow is less obvious to the user and would require the extension to teach the user to do it. When this global toggle is used, the permission request could follow the regular flow as documented (SUMO docs, extension workshop docs). I expect extension developers to prefer the flow you've described before (\"checkbox in permission prompt\") over the one from this paragraph (\"global toggle\"). \n\n--> is this it or is there more to it?",
      "reactions": [],
      "client_meta": {
        "node_id": "7120:7574",
        "node_offset": {
          "x": 654,
          "y": 211
        },
        "stable_path": [
          "7120:7574"
        ]
      },
      "order_id": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "955768527",
      "uuid": "31fdd20b-8724-4865-8b32-c4e08c82ebf1",
      "file_key": "MXbHIPIMANW2iIMvHeCSCR",
      "parent_id": "",
      "user": {
        "handle": "Rob Wu",
        "img_url": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cd0b13a6822ca45956e86bd365893791?size=240&default=https%3A%2F%2Fs3-alpha.figma.com%2Fstatic%2Fuser_r_v2.png",
        "id": "1009075524053089511"
      },
      "created_at": "2024-09-19T14:25:05.270Z",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "message": "This mock shows only one permission. This is the desired flow for requesting the necessary permissions.\n\nIn practice, extensions can request any number of permissions. In this case, the desired outcome is that users can click Allow to grant the other requested permissions, and that the userScripts permission is not granted if the user did not check it.",
      "reactions": [],
      "client_meta": {
        "node_id": "7120:7539",
        "node_offset": {
          "x": 1020,
          "y": 165
        },
        "stable_path": [
          "7120:7539"
        ]
      },
      "order_id": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "955761585",
      "uuid": "155f8a59-1185-4eff-b9c5-a324a52b049d",
      "file_key": "MXbHIPIMANW2iIMvHeCSCR",
      "parent_id": "",
      "user": {
        "handle": "Rob Wu",
        "img_url": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cd0b13a6822ca45956e86bd365893791?size=240&default=https%3A%2F%2Fs3-alpha.figma.com%2Fstatic%2Fuser_r_v2.png",
        "id": "1009075524053089511"
      },
      "created_at": "2024-09-19T14:21:43.913Z",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "message": "When the checkbox is unchecked, the outcome of clicking \"Allow\" would not be obvious to users who did not notice the checkbox.\n\nThe only case where ignoring is desirable is when the user does not understand the request and was just mindlessly clicking through.\n\nIn other cases, where the user intended to grant permissions, the effect of \"Allow\" not actually allowing could potentially be confusing. A way to improve for this situation is to e.g. disable the Allow button until the checkbox is checked.\n\nA potential \"downside\" to disabling the button is that users may mindlessly try to click the checkbox to get the button unlocked, an affordance that they are taught from the many existing forms on the internet. I don't know whether this downside outweighs the benefits of reduces confusion in the cases where the user wants to grant permissions.",
      "reactions": [],
      "client_meta": {
        "node_id": "7120:7539",
        "node_offset": {
          "x": 1190,
          "y": 259
        },
        "stable_path": [
          "7120:7539"
        ]
      },
      "order_id": "2"
    },
    {
      "id": "955486290",
      "uuid": "c9214ebf-048c-4151-b358-2b6488ee45a3",
      "file_key": "MXbHIPIMANW2iIMvHeCSCR",
      "parent_id": "",
      "user": {
        "handle": "Rob Wu",
        "img_url": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cd0b13a6822ca45956e86bd365893791?size=240&default=https%3A%2F%2Fs3-alpha.figma.com%2Fstatic%2Fuser_r_v2.png",
        "id": "1009075524053089511"
      },
      "created_at": "2024-09-19T11:52:19.936Z",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "message": "While this notice + CTA would make it easier for user script managers to convert users to the desired flow, I don't think that we need to implement this ourselves, at least not in the MVP. Extensions have the means to detect whether the userScripts permission was granted and then display their own custom UI to the user.",
      "reactions": [],
      "client_meta": {
        "node_id": "7120:7340",
        "node_offset": {
          "x": 1127,
          "y": 133
        },
        "stable_path": [
          "7120:7340"
        ]
      },
      "order_id": "1"
    }
  ],
